Use SO_TIMESTAMP, not SO_BINTIME, for packet timestamps on QNX.

QNX's io-sock defines SO_BINTIME and SCM_BINTIME (it is FreeBSD-derived),
but declares struct bintime only in its driver headers (<devs/sys/time.h>),
not for applications, so the BINTIME receive-timestamp path does not
compile. Take the next one ntp knows: SO_TIMESTAMP (struct timeval), which
QNX supports.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/ntpd/ntp_io.c
+++ b/ntpd/ntp_io.c
@@ -107,7 +107,7 @@
 nic_rule *nic_rule_list;
 
 
-#if defined(SO_BINTIME) && defined(SCM_BINTIME) && defined(CMSG_FIRSTHDR)
+#if defined(SO_BINTIME) && defined(SCM_BINTIME) && defined(CMSG_FIRSTHDR) && !defined(__QNX__)
 #  define HAVE_PACKET_TIMESTAMP
 #  define HAVE_BINTIME
 #  ifdef BINTIME_CTLMSGBUF_SIZE
